module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.Homology.DerivedCategory.SingleTriangle
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.LocalOnCover.Coherence
public import StacksFrontier.Algebra.Category.ModuleCat.Sheaf.SiteCoherenceFiniteFamilies
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.PseudoCoherentSites.IteratedSliceFiniteType
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.PseudoCoherentSites.RestrictionGeneral
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.PseudoCoherentSites.SingleApproximationGeneral
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Topologies.CoherentKernelOnSite
public import StacksFrontier.AlgebraicGeometry.Topologies.ModuleTypesOnSite
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Cohomology.PseudoCoherent.LocalNature
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Cohomology.PseudoCoherent.ShiftAndVanishing
public import StacksFrontier.CategoryTheory.Sites.Cohomology.PseudoCoherent.Summands

/-!
# Coherent modules on a ringed site with coherent structure sheaf are pseudo-coherent

Let `(C, O)` be a ringed site whose structure sheaf is coherent and let `M` be a coherent
`O`-module. Placed in degree zero, `M` is an `m`-pseudo-coherent object of `D(O)` for every
integer `m`.

This is the sheaf-theoretic form of the statement that over a Noetherian ring a finite module
has a resolution by finite free modules of unbounded length, and it is what the truncation
induction `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isPseudoCoherentFrom_of_homology_of_boundedAbove_general`
(the bounded above case of Stacks 09V8) consumes to prove the implication (1) ⇒ (2) of
Stacks 08IK.

## Main results

* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isDirectSummandOfFiniteFree_free_of_finite`: a free module on a
  finite index type is a direct summand of a finite free module.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isPseudoCoherentFrom_singleFunctor_zero_free`: a free module on
  a finite index type, placed in degree zero, is `m`-pseudo-coherent for every `m`.
* `AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isPseudoCoherentFrom_singleFunctor_zero_of_isCoherentOnSite`:
  on a ringed site with coherent structure sheaf, a coherent module placed in degree zero is
  `m`-pseudo-coherent for every `m`.

## The syzygy induction

The argument is the one of the scheme-level
`AlgebraicGeometry.isPseudoCoherentFrom_singleFunctor_zero_of_isCoherent`, run on the slices of
the site rather than on open subschemes. For `m ≥ 1` there is nothing to prove, a module in
degree zero having no cohomology in degrees `≥ 1`
(`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isPseudoCoherentFrom_of_isLE`). For the step,
pseudo-coherence is local on the site (Stacks 08FU (3),
`AlgebraicGeometry.RingedSite.isPseudoCoherentFrom_of_locally_general`), so it suffices to work
on the members of a covering of each object `U`. Finite type of `M|_U` supplies a covering
sieve of `U` over each arrow `V ⟶ U` of which `M|_V` is generated by finitely many global
sections (`Stacks.SheafOfModules.exists_covering_sieve_finite_generators_of_isFiniteType_over`),
that is, receives an epimorphism `π : 𝒪_V^{⊕I} ⟶ M|_V` with `I` finite. The free module is
coherent (Stacks 01BY (3), direct sums), so is `M|_V`
(`Stacks.SheafOfModules.isCoherentOnSite_over`), hence so is `Ker(π)` (Stacks 01BY (3),
kernels), and the inductive hypothesis applies to it on the localized ringed site
`(C/V, O|_V)`, whose structure sheaf is again coherent. The short exact sequence
`0 → Ker(π) → 𝒪_V^{⊕I} → M|_V → 0` is a distinguished triangle of `D(O|_V)` and Stacks 08FV (1)
concludes.

The induction therefore quantifies over all ringed sites at once: its step replaces the site by
a slice and the module by a kernel which lives only on that slice. The slice of a ringed site
`RingedSite.{max u v, v, w}` is again one, so the ringed sites are taken at those universes.

## Implementation notes

The derived categories are the standard ones, `HasDerivedCategory.standard`, on the site and on
all of its slices alike, as in
`StacksFrontier/AlgebraicGeometry/Spaces/PseudoCoherent/CoherentCohomology.lean`, where this is
used for the small étale site of an algebraic space.

